# DealMemory™

> **Product Memory & Real-Time Activation Engine for SaaS and AI Agents**

DealMemory sits between **product analytics** and **customer memory**. Analytics tools tell you *how many* users did something; DealMemory reveals *what a specific user did* and *what context your team should remember*.

When a user signs up, opens the dashboard, creates a project, tries to connect an integration, then stops, DealMemory reconstructs that exact sequence as an interactive timeline. The founder can immediately see the user's last recorded action and diagnose where onboarding broke, without digging through server logs or guessing from generic dashboard metrics.

Built with Next.js 14, PostgreSQL, Prisma ORM, and a console design inspired by [Supermemory.ai](https://supermemory.ai).

---

## The simplest setup

DealMemory is built around one idea: one package, one key, one init command — on both the customer app side and the console side.

**Customer app side**

```bash
npm install dealmemory
npx dealmemory init

# .env
DEALMEMORY_API_KEY=pk_live_your_project_key
```

After that, `import 'dealmemory/auto'` starts tracking page views, clicks, sessions, and common product milestones automatically. The CLI writes the bootstrap into the app for you, and the SDK reads everything it needs from the environment. There is no project id to pass in code, no manual apiUrl to configure, and no config file to hand-write.

**Console / dashboard side**

The Dashboard is part of this app at `app/`. Once DealMemory is deployed and a team signs in, the console is already ready: keys, projects, ingestion, and the dashboard are all wired. The same “one key, one install” idea applies on the console side — the team running DealMemory opens a dashboard that is already live after signup.

If your goal is the simplest possible shipping story, that is the model: one product, one key, one install, one console that comes up immediately after signup.

